Flask-Migrate / Alembic tooling for this app (CLAUDE-P27-B).

Adopted at the point models.py's own docstring already flagged: "a
third genuine schema change should prompt revisiting real migration
tooling rather than growing this by hand again." That third change is
the tenancy/project-authorization work specified (not yet built) in
governance/specified-unbuilt/tenancy-and-project-authorization.md.

How this coexists with app.py's existing hand-rolled migrations
--------------------------------------------------------------
app.py's `_register_database()` still runs `db.create_all()` plus two
hand-written idempotent functions (`_migrate_users_email_column`,
`_migrate_users_email_case_insensitive_index`, `_migrate_users_is_
active_column`) on every boot -- THIS IS UNCHANGED by adopting Flask-
Migrate. Those functions are what actually creates/upgrades the
already-deployed schema today; nothing here replaces them.

Because `create_all()` always runs first (even when Flask is booted
via the `flask db` CLI, since it loads through the same app factory),
`flask db migrate`'s autogenerate can never see a diff against an
already-correct database -- it always reports "No changes in schema
detected." versions/f8620fa70917_baseline_schema_claude_p27_b_users_.py
was therefore hand-written, not autogenerated, and verified (CLAUDE-
P27-B session) to produce byte-identical `sqlite_master` schema text
to what `create_all()` + the hand-migrations actually produce on a
fresh database.

The already-deployed instance/bhive.db was `flask db stamp head`-ed to
this baseline revision -- NOT upgraded through it -- so its
`alembic_version` table records "already at this schema" without
re-running any DDL against live data.

Going forward
-------------
The NEXT real schema change (starting with the tenancy work above)
should be a normal Alembic revision (`flask db migrate` / hand-written
if autogenerate again sees no diff for the reason above, then `flask
db upgrade`), not another hand-written ALTER TABLE function in app.py.
